# pmx_ff.manifest -- what pmx adds to, or must preserve in, each bundled force field.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------
# Every residue and parameter pmx introduced used to live inside a *vendored*
# file (merged.rtp, aminoacids.rtp, ffbonded.itp), indistinguishable from
# upstream content.  On 2026-07-15 a revert of merged.rtp silently took the
# CN0/KN0/KS0 NOS endpoints, 7 acyl-lysines and NME with it; nothing noticed
# until 2026-08-05, and recovering them cost a day of archaeology through build
# artifacts.  Nothing in the tree could answer "which residues did we add, and
# are they all still here?".
#
# This file answers that question, and tests/test_ff_manifest.py enforces it.
# A revert or regeneration that drops an entry now fails a test instead of
# surfacing weeks later as a confusing build error.
#
# FORMAT
# ------
#   <force-field>  <kind>  <value>  [parent]
#
# Blank lines and #-comments ignored.  Kinds:
#
#   residue   RTP entry that must resolve through ffparser.load_ff_rtp.
#             `parent` is the file it is expected to live in (informational --
#             the check is that it resolves, not where from, since a residue may
#             legitimately move between the base RTP and a topic file).
#   charge    residue whose net charge must equal `parent` (to 1e-4).  Guards the
#             ECC scaling: charges are a silent failure mode -- a wrong one still
#             builds, still runs, and only shows up as a bad free energy.
#   hdb       residue that must have a hydrogen-build entry in some *.hdb.
#   file      file that must exist in the force-field directory.
#   include   file that must exist AND be #include'd by `parent`.
#   restype   residue that must be classified in the tree-level residuetypes.dat
#             (force-field column is '-').  A per-force-field residuetypes.dat
#             REPLACES this file rather than merging with it, so introducing one
#             silently declassifies everything it omits -- that is exactly what a
#             12-byte stub containing only "6FL Protein" did to charmm36m-mut.
#
# ADDING TO THIS FILE
# -------------------
# If you hand-add a residue or parameter block to a force field, add a line here
# in the same commit.  If it is upstream content being restored, add it too --
# the point is to detect loss, not only to record authorship.

# ── charmm36m-mut ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOS / SONOS bound-state endpoints.  Ours: present in no upstream, reconstructed
# 2026-08-06 from the state-B columns of topologies that ran.  These are what
# `pmx mutate --react` resolves; a hybrid library cannot supply them.
charmm36m-mut       residue   CN0            nos.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   KN0            nos.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   KS0            nos.rtp
charmm36m-mut       include   nos_bonded.itp ffbonded.itp
charmm36m-mut       file      cgenff_link.itp

# Acyl-lysines.  Upstream content that had been lost locally; restored to a topic
# file 2026-08-06.  The histone-reader showcase depends on these.
charmm36m-mut       residue   ALY            acyl.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   BLY            acyl.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   FLY            acyl.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   HIB            acyl.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   KCR            acyl.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   PLY            acyl.rtp
charmm36m-mut       residue   SLY            acyl.rtp

# NME.  Upstream content, lost and restored.  It must live in merged.rtp: from a
# topic file pdb2gmx resolves the entry but then dies at atom-sorting with
# "Atom H in residue NME was not found in rtp entry NME with 6 atoms".  Needed by
# the GACE reference arm (ACE-ALA-X-ALA-NME).
charmm36m-mut       residue   NME            merged.rtp
charmm36m-mut       hdb       NME            merged.hdb

# ── charmm36m-ecc-mut ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Protein<->CGenFF link terms + the sulfenic/NOS bonded block.  CSO is a hybrid
# residue (CA=CT1 protein, CB=CG321 CGenFF), so it needs both.
charmm36m-ecc-mut   file      cgenff_link.itp
charmm36m-ecc-mut   include   cgenff_link.itp ffbonded.itp
charmm36m-ecc-mut   residue   GACE           merged.rtp
charmm36m-ecc-mut   residue   TYM            merged.rtp
charmm36m-ecc-mut   residue   NME            merged.rtp

# ── amber -q-ecc variants ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GACE is the GSH reference cap; ALY the acetyl-lysine.  Both turn out to be
# UPSTREAM content, not ours -- an earlier draft assumed otherwise because they
# sit indented mid-file in aminoacids.rtp (ALY at ~line 544), which reads like a
# hand-insertion.  Declared here anyway: the GSH reference work depends on GACE,
# so its disappearance must fail a test regardless of who authored it.
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut         residue GACE  aminoacids.rtp
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut         residue ALY   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut         residue GACE  aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut         residue ALY   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut residue GACE aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut residue ALY  aminoacids.rtp

# ── disulfide endpoints ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CHARMM ships CYS2, Amber ships CYX; covalent/target.py maps between them.  A
# recipe naming either endpoint must resolve on every one of these.
charmm36m-mut               residue CYS2  merged.rtp
charmm36m-ecc-mut           residue CYS2  merged.rtp
amber14sb-q-mut             residue CYX   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-mut             residue CYX   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sbmut                residue CYX   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-mut  residue CYX   aminoacids.rtp

# ── tree-level residuetypes.dat ──────────────────────────────────────────────
-  restype  CN0   Protein
-  restype  KN0   Protein
-  restype  KS0   Protein
-  restype  6FL   Protein
-  restype  NME   Protein
-  restype  ACE   Protein

# ── TYM (deprotonated tyrosine) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The ONLY residue pmx adds across the entire Amber family, and it is in
# charmm36m-ecc-mut too.  GACE and ALY, which an earlier draft of
# FF_LAYOUT_PROPOSAL.md claimed were ours, turned out to be upstream content.
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut             residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber14sb-q-mut                 residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber14sbmut                    residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut             residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-mut                 residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sbmut                    residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildn-mut         residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut  residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-mut      residue TYM   aminoacids.rtp
charmm36m-ecc-mut               residue TYM   merged.rtp

# ── Amber phospho residues ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Live in the vendored aminoacids.rtp and are correctly ECC-scaled: SP1/TP1/YP1
# -0.75 and SP2/TP2/YP2 -1.50, i.e. 0.75x per formal charge unit.  Declared here
# because CHARMM's ECC force field does NOT scale its equivalents (SEP -1,
# TPO -2, PTR -1) -- Amber is the precedent for fixing that.
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut             residue SP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut             residue SP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut             residue TP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut             residue TP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut             residue YP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber14sb-q-ecc-mut             residue YP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut             residue SP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut             residue SP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut             residue TP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut             residue TP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut             residue YP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber19sb-q-ecc-mut             residue YP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut  residue SP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut  residue SP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut  residue TP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut  residue TP2   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut  residue YP1   aminoacids.rtp
amber99sb-star-ildnp-q-ecc-mut  residue YP2   aminoacids.rtp

# ── DEAD FILES -- do not trust as a source ───────────────────────────────────
# *.r2tp, *.r3tp and *.m2tp (phosaa.r2tp, phosaa.r3tp, mutres.r2tp, mutres.m2tp,
# aminoacids.r2tp) are read by NOTHING: not pmx (ffparser globs *.rtp only) and
# not pdb2gmx (same).  They are upstream files, left in place, but they are dead.
# phosaa.r2tp in particular looks authoritative -- it carries live-looking,
# correctly-scaled SP1/SP2/TP1/TP2/YP1/YP2 blocks -- while the entries actually
# in use come from aminoacids.rtp.  Do not edit a .r2tp expecting an effect.

# ── ECC charge scaling (charmm36m-ecc-mut) ───────────────────────────────────
# `kind = charge` pins a residue's net charge; the value column is the expected
# net.  charmm36m-ecc-mut scaled its canonical charged residues (x0.75 on every
# side-chain atom, backbone untouched) but NOT its PTMs, so SEP/TPO/PTR/MLY/M3L
# and the phosaa-named SP1..YP2 all still carried full formal charge.  Any ECC
# phospho or methyl calculation was mixing scaled protein charges with an
# unscaled modification -- TPO at -2.0 being the worst case.
#
# Scaled 2026-08-06 using the scheme derived from LYS/ASP (every non-backbone
# atom x0.75, original recorded as a trailing "; qold=" comment).  CHARMM's
# backbone sums to zero, so the net becomes 0.75 x the formal charge, matching
# Amber's phosaa set.  These lines are the regression guard.
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  SEP   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  TPO   -1.50
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  PTR   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  SP1   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  SP2   -1.50
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  TP1   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  TP2   -1.50
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  YP1   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  YP2   -1.50
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  MLY   +0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  M3L   +0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  MLZ   +0.75
# canonical residues, pinned so the two sets cannot drift apart
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  LYS   +0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  ARG   +0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  ASP   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  GLU   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  HSP   +0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  CYM   -0.75
charmm36m-ecc-mut  charge  TYM   -0.75
# NOS endpoints: NOT scaled, deliberately.  CN0/KN0 carry no formal charge --
# they are a bound state whose pair sums to zero (-0.154 / +0.154), so there is
# no ionic group to scale.  Pinned so nobody "fixes" them later.
charmm36m-mut      charge  CN0   -0.154
charmm36m-mut      charge  KN0   +0.154
charmm36m-mut      charge  KS0   +0.286

# ── UNRESOLVED: D-amino acids are still unscaled ─────────────────────────────
# DASP DGLU DLYS DARG DHSP carry full formal charge in charmm36m-ecc-mut while
# their L counterparts are scaled.  Same class of inconsistency as the PTMs
# above, but out of scope for the PTM work and not deliberately reviewed -- left
# for a decision rather than silently changed.  Not pinned here, because pinning
# the current (wrong-looking) values would entrench them.
